   "name": "Expanding Brackets",
   "definition": null,
   "video_id": "822959585"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Expanding brackets"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What is a polynomial?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- A **polynomial** is an algebraic expression consisting of a finite number of terms, with **non-negative integer** indices only"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/05/2.5.2-Polynomial-not-polynomial.png",
   "file": "assets/daaf557aa4e8-2-5-2-polynomial-not-polynomial.png",
   "alt": "examples of what is and isnt a polynomial",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What do I need to know about expanding brackets?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- **Expanding brackets** is now extended to include\n\n  - brackets with more than 2 terms\n  - brackets that are **polynomials** (beyond linear)\n- To expand two brackets, **each term** in the **first** set of brackets must be **multiplied** by **each term** in the **second** set of brackets\n\n  - If there are more than 2 brackets to expand, only work with a pair at a time\n  - You may have seen or used FOIL to expand quadratic expressions\n  \n    - that is helpful but is a special case (shortcut) of this more general approach"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/05/2.5.1-Expanding-Brackets-Illustration.png",
   "file": "assets/91c58b5ca0c6-2-5-1-expanding-brackets-illustration.png",
   "alt": "expanding brackets example",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- If you are trying to expand a two term bracket to a higher power (i.e. `(a+b)^n`) then use the **binomial expansion**"
  },
